WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:06.240
On the 19th of June the oceangate submersible
began its descent to the Titanic’s fractured

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:09.580
remains here, in the north western atlantic.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:14.519
With a descent rate of 55 metres per minute
it would take the submersible 70 minutes to

00:00:14.519 --> 00:00:16.530
reach its destination.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:21.779
With each passing minute the equivalent of
5.3 atmospheres of pressure would be added

00:00:21.779 --> 00:00:23.480
to the subs hull.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:29.300
By the time it reached its target depth the
weight of 366 of earth’s atmospheres would

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.210
be attempting to crush it.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:35.920
Applied to just an area a little larger than
an A3 piece of paper, that’s equivalent

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:39.170
to the thrust of a Saturn V F1 engine.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.170
Pressing in from all directions.

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:44.140
An hour and 45 minutes into the dive.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:46.110
At the bottom of the Ocean.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.830
3800 metres down.

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:50.530
With this immense pressure bearing down on
its hull.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:54.640
The oceangate submersible lost contact with
the surface.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:59.129
We now know the submersible imploded, a sudden
catastrophic collapse.

00:00:59.129 --> 00:01:05.059
A tragic accident, however any composite material
engineer could have predicted this failure,

00:01:05.059 --> 00:01:06.430
and they did.

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:11.580
I worked as a composite design engineer and
completed my master’s thesis in composite

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:12.600
failure prediction.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:18.210
So, let’s dive into the questionable engineering
decisions that Oceangate made that led to

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:19.700
a tragic accident.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:23.590
With that pressure comes some extreme design
challenges.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:29.180
Oceangate’s Titan submersible consists of
two titanium end caps, one with a large acrylic

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:33.970
window, joined to a filament wound carbon
fiber reinforced epoxy hull.

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:34.970
[REF]

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:37.220
Inside the submersible is pretty barebone.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:40.530
Some screens that are fed data from cameras
from the outside.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:45.170
The four electric thrusters are controlled
with, from what I can tell, a third party

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:47.310
gamecube controller.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:52.890
Many people are focusing on this part of the
design, but that’s the least of my concerns.

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:58.490
We will be focusing on one design aspect of
this submersible, the composite hull.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:02.150
Carbon fibre composites aren’t really known
for their compressive strength.

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:03.840
They work best in tension.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.840
Great for airplanes that are pressurised from
the inside.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.530
Where the pressure inside the fuselage works
to expand the circular cross section, putting

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.560
the fibres in tension.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:18.200
For a submarine, the pressure will work to
compress the hull, placing the fibres primarily

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:19.500
under compression.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:24.170
This immediately set off alarm bells in my
mind when I heard of the missing submarine,

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:29.110
so I began digging into their justification
for using the material.

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:34.220
Oceangate chose this carbon fibre composite
primarily to benefit from its natural buoyancy.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:39.040
These types of subs actually want to be as
close to neutrally buoyant as possible.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.670
Meaning the weight of water they displace
weighs about the same as the vehicle itself.

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:47.560
Minimising the energy needed to rise or sink
in the water.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:52.220
This means to surface in an emergency they
don’t need power, they just drop ballasts

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:56.230
that make them positively buoyant and they
just float to the surface.

00:02:56.230 --> 00:03:00.390
The carbon fibre composite can be lighter
and thus helps achieve this neutral buoyancy

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:01.720
target.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:06.819
Typically submarine hulls are made from steel
or more recently titanium, but in order to

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:11.090
achieve the desired buoyancy they need foam
outer layers.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:16.060
OceanGates CEO Stockton Rush, who was inside
the submersible stated that they wanted to

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:19.860
skip this foam layer because it added to costs.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:24.890
That isn’t necessarily an evil thing if
the composite is up for the job.

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:30.420
This is where things get iffy, oceangate had
no idea whether it was up to the task or not,

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:35.780
and we know this because they admit in their
own blog post justifying their decision to

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:38.939
not test their vehicle with a regulatory body.

00:03:38.939 --> 00:03:39.939
[REF].

00:03:39.939 --> 00:03:42.030
The now removed blog entry read:

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:46.460
Most major marine operators require that chartered
vessels are “classed” by an independent

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:49.709
group
Classing assures ship owners, insurers, and

00:03:49.709 --> 00:03:55.480
regulators that vessels are designed, constructed
and inspected to accepted standards.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:00.180
Classing may be effective at filtering out
unsatisfactory designers and builders, but

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:05.420
the established standards do little to weed
out subpar vessel operators The vast majority

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:11.239
of marine (and aviation) accidents are a result
of operator error, not mechanical failure.

00:04:11.239 --> 00:04:16.380
As a result, simply focusing on classing the
vessel does not address the operational risks.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:20.970
Now, I would have thought this wouldn’t
need pointing out, but the fact this glaring

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:26.020
red flag has been written on their own website
for everyone to see, and several extremely

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:30.760
wealthy and successful people didn’t seem
to think it was an issue seems to suggest

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:32.060
otherwise.

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:37.280
Perhaps most submarine failures occur due
to operator error precisely because they have

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:38.910
undergone rigorous testing and certification.

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:45.039
The comparison to aviation is also just a
wild can of worms that we have very recent

00:04:45.039 --> 00:04:50.540
catastrophic accidents to compare to, where
manufacturers willfully circumvented regulatory

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:51.729
procedures.

00:04:51.729 --> 00:04:56.210
These aren’t procedures created as some
kind of unnecessary inconvenience to inventors.

00:04:56.210 --> 00:05:01.250
They are very useful guidelines and testing
procedures to prove you have not missed some

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:06.190
catastrophic failure mode in your design,
and that’s exactly what happened.

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:10.919
One failure mode is unique to composite materials
in deep sea applications.

00:05:10.919 --> 00:05:15.910
Some of the first evidence we got for it was
glass fibre reinforced deep sea piping.

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:20.630
It’s called snap buckling, or delamination
coupled buckling failure mode.

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:24.750
For thin walled pressure vessels the failure
mode is simply buckling, where the pressure

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:29.890
simply caves the entire wall in, but here,
because the wall thickness is so large to

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:34.729
deal with the immense pressure of the deep
sea the failure mode gets more complicated.

00:05:34.729 --> 00:05:40.050
This failure mode is characterised by delamination
of the internal layer of the pressure vessel,

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:44.680
basically the inside of the pressure vessel
suddenly peels away from the rest of the wall,

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:48.340
leading to catastrophic failure of the overall
structure.

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:54.470
The exact mechanisms of the failure mode are
not entirely well understood, as stated in

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:59.729
this 2022 research paper titled “A Review
on Structural Failure of Composite Pressure

00:05:59.729 --> 00:06:00.760
Hulls in Deep Sea”.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:02.870
[REF] It states, in its conclusion

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:07.050
“investigations on this subject have remained
at theoretical level.

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:10.650
There is still very little research in this
area in recent years.”

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:16.560
So, I am assuming whoever engineered this
pressure vessel did at least the same level

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:20.590
of research as a YouTuber who wrote a script
on the subject in two days.

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:24.080
They must have at least been aware of this
problem.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:29.680
A failure mode like this is going to be exacerbated
by gradual damage incurred through cyclical

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:34.930
stresses, like those experienced when diving
to the depths of 4000 metres and resurfacing

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:35.930
again.

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:40.389
They have done that at least 50 times with
this vessel, without ever fatigue testing

00:06:40.389 --> 00:06:41.389
it.

00:06:41.389 --> 00:06:46.180
Per their own admission they did not undergo
industry standard classifications, but they

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:48.819
did do their own classification test.

00:06:48.819 --> 00:06:51.930
One test, stated on that blog post once again.

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:57.390
“A licensed marine surveyor will witness
a successful dive to 4000 metres, inspect

00:06:57.390 --> 00:07:02.400
the vessel before and after the dive, and
provide a Statement of Fact attesting to the

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:04.629
completion of the dive test plan.”

00:07:04.629 --> 00:07:07.900
That is simply not adequate.

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:11.970
Anything undergoing repeated stress cycles
like this needs to be fatigue tested.

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:17.080
Like the test airplane manufacturers do on
wings where they flex the plane's wings thousands

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:19.130
of times on the ground.

00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:23.960
They designed this thing essentially with
computer modelling with no real world testing.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:28.170
Composite materials are incredibly difficult
to model in computer software.

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:33.060
Composite materials are far more complicated
than homogeneous materials like steel, where

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:38.200
the material properties are the same in all
directions and in all regions of the material.

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:44.280
Composites are composed of fibre bound together
by a matrix material like epoxy.

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:48.810
If we were to zoom into a small cross section
of this, we may see something like this.

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:53.270
Some fibres, some of them could be touching
without any matrix between them, some could

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:57.659
be spaced further apart, and there can also
be empty voids that the epoxy didn’t manage

00:07:57.659 --> 00:07:59.039
to fill.

00:07:59.039 --> 00:08:03.410
We can simulate a small section like this
independently to help analyse some failure

00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:05.599
modes, such as delamination.

00:08:05.599 --> 00:08:10.640
However if we want to zoom out and test a
larger section we often need to average the

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:15.080
material properties in some way or else the
simulation will take too long to run.

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:20.780
My own master thesis tried to combine the
two by embedding a fibre and matrix cell within

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:23.240
an averaged composite material.

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:27.590
There are specialised softwares that can model
different fibre orientations, but they are

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:30.410
still very much an estimation.

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:33.149
You need to back this up with physical testing.

00:08:33.149 --> 00:08:35.630
Something OceanGate didn’t do.

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:38.680
There are more signs of incompetent use of
composites.

00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:43.399
For a critical structure like this, I would
very much recommend curing it in an autoclave.

00:08:43.399 --> 00:08:48.080
That’s basically a pressurised oven that
helps push those voids I mentioned earlier

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:50.120
out of the epoxy resin.

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:54.600
Autoclaves are expensive, and can be difficult
to find ones large enough to fit something

00:08:54.600 --> 00:09:00.399
like this, but it’s nice to not have air
bubbles in a pressure vessel keeping you alive.

00:09:00.399 --> 00:09:04.600
However, it wasn’t used for this pressure
vessel.

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:08.250
It was simply bagged and cured, a cheaper
alternative.

00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:12.720
Spencer Composites was commissioned to make
the composite hull and their CEO was interviewed

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:16.690
back in 2017 in a compositesworld article.

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:21.320
He seems very aware of the immense design
challenge he was handed, and he does mention

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:25.990
that the porosity of the cured pressure vessel
was assessed to be less than &lt;1%.

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:30.519
“Less than 1% voids” doesn’t exactly
inspire confidence.

00:09:30.519 --> 00:09:35.149
Is it 0.99% or 0.0001% porosity?

00:09:35.149 --> 00:09:40.380
That’s exactly the type of defect that’s
going to increase the risk of snap buckling

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:44.320
as voids can be propagation sites for delamination.

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:49.930
The blog post goes on to dismiss classification
tests as unsuitable for innovative designs,

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:53.290
which is just complete narcissistic nonsense.

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:54.610
This isn’t innovative.

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:59.470
They have just made the pressure vessel from
a carbon fibre composite, using known manufacturing

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:05.640
practices, and started charging rich people
250,000 dollars to risk their lives in it.

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:08.790
Innovation would be creating that pressure
vessel and testing it.

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:14.220
Figuring out the causes of snap buckling and
advancing material science for all mankind.

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:17.240
This is profiteering, not innovation.

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:22.320
To eliminate all doubt that this company has
willfully ignored standard safety practices.

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:28.630
This court case from 2018 was brought against
oceangate by a former employee who was fired

00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:32.630
shortly after raising concerns about the composite
hull.

00:10:32.630 --> 00:10:37.100
Stating not enough was done to check for delaminations
and voids.

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:41.670
Oceangate argued that the technology to check
for voids and delaminations in a composite

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:47.329
this thick did not exist, and instead they
would use their acoustic monitoring system,

00:10:47.329 --> 00:10:51.149
which essentially listens for the sounds of
fracture to predict failure.

00:10:51.149 --> 00:10:54.960
But this is what a carbon fibre composite
fracture curve looks like.

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:56.490
It’s a cliff edge.

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:58.420
Sudden catastrophic collapse.

00:10:58.420 --> 00:11:03.250
An acoustic monitoring system like this is
akin to setting up a camera to warn you thunder

00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:04.250
is coming.

00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:09.410
You will see the lighting before the thunder,
but the time between them is minimal.

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:11.389
Pure safety theatre.

00:11:11.389 --> 00:11:12.829
It gets even worse.

00:11:12.829 --> 00:11:17.730
On one test dive in the Bahamas, a submersible
expert could hear the cracking with his own

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:22.269
ears and warned Stockton Rush that it was
a sign of damage accumulating.

00:11:22.269 --> 00:11:26.570
Stockton, once again, chose to ignore an expert's
advice.

00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:30.930
So this acoustic monitoring system was purely
for marketing purposes.

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:35.350
To give customers, who don’t have the engineering
background to see through it, a false sense

00:11:35.350 --> 00:11:36.550
of security.

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:41.760
I would attribute this to malice and greed,
but Stockton Rush chose to pilot this death

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:43.279
trap himself.

00:11:43.279 --> 00:11:47.430
As the Hanlon’s Razor goes:
Never attribute to malice that which can be

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:49.640
attributed to stupidity.

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:53.889
There is this growing trend in engineering
of moving fast and breaking stuff.

00:11:53.889 --> 00:12:00.120
Mostly driven by one overconfident CEO who
has, to be fair to him, succeeded in revolutionising

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:03.339
an entire industry with that ethos.

00:12:03.339 --> 00:12:08.410
That incriminating blog post even mentions
SpaceX, Blue Origin and Virgin Galactic as

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:12.620
bastions of innovation for this move fast
and break things mantra.

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:17.889
Virgin Galactic too have blood on their hands,
with their 2014 in-flight break up, which

00:12:17.889 --> 00:12:23.090
was attributed to inadequate design safeguards
and poor pilot training, along with a lack

00:12:23.090 --> 00:12:26.070
of oversight from regulatory authorities.

00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:30.720
This attitude is dangerous, and this won’t
be the last story of wealthy narcissistic

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:33.120
CEO’s putting lives at risk.

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:38.420
When a doctor is negligent to the safety of
their patients, they are liable for malpractice,

00:12:38.420 --> 00:12:43.490
but the impact their incompetence has affects
one patient, when an engineer neglects their

00:12:43.490 --> 00:12:48.990
responsibilities it can affect hundreds, thousands,
maybe even millions of people.

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:53.430
We need more people like David Lockridge,
the employee who was fired for raising concerns

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:54.820
over safety.

00:12:54.820 --> 00:12:58.350
The very first video on this channel stated
our mission.

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:04.399
To inspire the next generation of engineers,
but it’s more than just inspiring engineers,

00:13:04.399 --> 00:13:08.060
we need engineers going into the most impactful
industries.

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:11.240
To solve the largest problems facing our civilization.

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:16.330
You’ve probably heard a lot of career advice:
‘follow your passion’, ‘do what you

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:18.130
love’, ‘take the initiative’, etc. etc.

00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:22.190
But a lot of the advice out there doesn’t
take into account the best available evidence

00:13:22.190 --> 00:13:26.660
we have for what actually makes for a high
impact and fulfilling career.

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:33.220
Today's sponsor, 80,000 Hours is a nonprofit
that, for the last ten years, has been researching

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:38.380
the question of how you can find a fulfilling
career that does good, too.

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:42.589
Everything they publish is based on careful
consideration of the data, consultation with

00:13:42.589 --> 00:13:47.860
experts, and taking into account the best
evidence they could find on the topic.

00:13:47.860 --> 00:13:52.890
And because they take this evidence-based
approach, they are able to make concrete recommendations

00:13:52.890 --> 00:13:56.649
on hard questions like:
Where should I work if I actually want to

00:13:56.649 --> 00:13:58.610
make a positive difference in the world?

00:13:58.610 --> 00:14:03.959
What are the most pressing problems facing
humanity today, and how might we solve them?

00:14:03.959 --> 00:14:07.959
What can I do today to start planning a fulfilling
career?

00:14:07.959 --> 00:14:13.720
They have published over 30 articles on one
incredible pressing issue, safety in the development

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:15.150
of AI.

00:14:15.150 --> 00:14:20.209
They have a website with tons of collected
research on high-impact careers, a job board

00:14:20.209 --> 00:14:25.160
which lists currently open roles they think
might help you have a positive impact, and

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:29.449
a podcast where they have conversations with
experts about the world’s most pressing

00:14:29.449 --> 00:14:30.870
problems.

00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:35.949
Everything they provide is free, forever,
because they’re a nonprofit - their only

00:14:35.949 --> 00:14:41.820
aim is to help solve global problems by helping
people find the most impactful careers they

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:42.820
can.

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:47.060
If you join the newsletter now, you’ll get
a free copy of their in-depth research-led

00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:54.709
career guide sent to your inbox - just sign
up at 80000hours.org/realengineering, or click

00:14:54.709 --> 00:14:54.880
the button on screen now

